// A grammar stage constrains generation and leaves the reply a stream.
//
// `?p |> g` sends `g`'s grammar with the request and carries its anchors along
// with the resulting stream. Printing that stream shows tokens as they arrive
// with the muted region suppressed; reading it as a value gives the full text,
// muted span included.
//
// The mute region is (anchor, stop): output stays visible until the model emits
// "<t>", is suppressed until "</t>", then becomes visible again. A grammar with
// no anchor mutes from the very first token instead, since the whole reply is
// then structured output.
//
// This used to be spelled `stream(?p, mute_on = [g])`. That builtin is gone as
// of v1.2.4: it existed only because a grammar-constrained dereference used to
// collapse into a blocking call, leaving no stream to print. It does not any
// more, so one operator covers both.

prompt p = "Reply with <t>a</t> and nothing else."

let g = Grammar.new("\"a\"|\"b\"", "<t>", "</t>")

// Live output, with the region between the anchors suppressed.
print(?p |> g)

// The same dereference read as a value carries the muted text too.
let out = ?p |> g
print(len(out) > 0)
